# CountsFor scheduled jobs. Replaces the GitHub Actions scrape workflow.
#
# Install as /etc/cron.d/countsfor. Cron skips files in /etc/cron.d whose name
# contains a dot, so drop the ".cron". The file must be owned by root, mode 644,
# and end with a newline.
#   sudo install -m 644 -o root -g root deploy/countsfor.cron /etc/cron.d/countsfor
#   sudo install -d -m 755 -o countsfor -g countsfor /var/log/countsfor
# Cron notices the change by itself; no reload is needed.
#
# TIME ZONE: cron uses the server's local time zone (check with `timedatectl`).
# The times below assume Asia/Qatar (UTC+3, no daylight saving): 15:00 in Qatar
# is 12:00 UTC, the old workflow's daily slot. On a server kept in UTC, write
# "0 12" instead of "0 15" (and "0 6" instead of "0 9" for the optional line).
# Restart cron after changing the server's time zone: sudo systemctl restart cron
#
# The scrape:
#   flock -n    skips this run if the previous one still holds the lock
#               (update_data.py also takes its own lock and exits 75 if busy)
#   timeout 3h  stops a hung run: TERM after 3 hours, KILL 5 minutes later
#   run_scrape.sh reads /etc/countsfor/scraper.env (deploy/scraper.env.example),
#               scrapes the Schedule of Classes, rebuilds and checks the catalog
#               in a staging folder, then publishes backend/data/soc.json and
#               frontend/data/courses.json(.gz). On any failure the live files
#               stay as they were. Exit 0 published or nothing to do, 1 failed,
#               75 another update running.
# Output goes to /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log (rotated by deploy/countsfor.logrotate).
# Check the last run: tail -n 50 /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log
# Run by hand:        sudo -u countsfor /var/www/html/countsfor/backend/scraper/run_scrape.sh
#                     (always as countsfor: run_scrape.sh refuses any other user)
# After a deploy:     sudo -u countsfor /var/www/html/countsfor/backend/scraper/run_scrape.sh --no-scrape
#
# FAILURES: the log is the only record. On a non-zero exit (1 failed, 75 busy,
# 124 or 137 stopped by timeout, 1 from flock when the previous run holds the
# lock) the "|| echo" after the redirect prints one line, and cron mails a job's
# output to MAILTO. With MAILTO="" (below) that line is dropped, so a failure is
# silent. To be told, install a mailer that can send from this server (ask IT
# for the campus relay) and set MAILTO to an admin address. Either way, check
# now and then that Admin, Overview shows a recent last scrape.
#
# Note: in a crontab line "%" means newline, so a literal % is written "\%".

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
# CHANGE_ME (optional): an admin address, once the server can send mail.
MAILTO=""

# m  h  dom mon dow  user       command
0 15 * * * countsfor /usr/bin/flock -n /run/lock/countsfor-scrape.lock /usr/bin/timeout -k 5m 3h /var/www/html/countsfor/backend/scraper/run_scrape.sh >> /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log 2>&1 || echo "CountsFor daily update failed (exit $?): see /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log"

# Optional second run on weekday mornings during registration (09:00 Qatar,
# 06:00 UTC), as the old workflow had:
# 0 9 * * 1-5 countsfor /usr/bin/flock -n /run/lock/countsfor-scrape.lock /usr/bin/timeout -k 5m 3h /var/www/html/countsfor/backend/scraper/run_scrape.sh >> /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log 2>&1 || echo "CountsFor daily update failed (exit $?): see /var/log/countsfor/scrape.log"

# Optional nightly database backup at 02:30, keeping 14 days. The countsfor OS
# user reaches its own database by peer authentication, so no password is needed.
# Copy the dumps off the server as well: a backup on the same disk does not
# survive the disk. Restore: pg_restore --no-owner -d countsfor <file>.dump
#   sudo install -d -m 700 -o countsfor -g countsfor /var/backups/countsfor
# 30 2 * * * countsfor /usr/bin/pg_dump -Fc -d countsfor -f /var/backups/countsfor/countsfor-$(date +\%F).dump >> /var/log/countsfor/backup.log 2>&1 && /usr/bin/find /var/backups/countsfor -name 'countsfor-*.dump' -mtime +14 -delete
